.TH "PCMRECORD" "1" "October 23, 2026" "ka9q-radio" "ka9q-radio Manual"
.SH NAME
pcmrecord \- record RTP streams or pipe them to other programs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pcmrecord
.RI "[options] data-destination"
.SH DESCRIPTION
pcmrecord receives RTP streams and their associated status metadata. By
default it records all selected sessions into separate files. PCM is stored in
WAVE files; big-endian samples are converted to little-endian. Opus packets
are wrapped in Ogg rather than decoded. --raw omits PCM file headers.
.PP
--stdout selects one stream and writes it to standard output. --exec starts a
shell command for each selected stream and writes that stream to the command's
standard input. These modes supersede file segmentation and naming options.
Use --ssrc to select a predictable stream rather than accepting the first one
received.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B "\-c, \-\-stdout, \-\-catmode"
Write one stream to standard output. Without --ssrc, select the first received
session.
.TP
.B "\-\-reacquire"
With --stdout, continue following the selected logical SSRC across
sender/session changes. Requires --stdout.
.TP
.B "\-e command, \-\-exec command"
Start a command per session using the shell, with substitutions listed below.
Supersedes --stdout.
.TP
.B "\-d directory, \-\-directory directory"
Set recording directory. It must already exist. Default current directory.
.TP
.B "\-S ssrc, \-\-ssrc ssrc"
Select one SSRC. Decimal and C-style integer prefixes are accepted.
.TP
.B "\-o source, \-\-source source"
Restrict reception to a network source.
.TP
.B "\-r, \-\-raw"
Omit WAVE headers for PCM. Ignored for Opus, which retains Ogg framing.
.TP
.B "\-f, \-\-flush"
Flush stream output promptly; for Opus, flush Ogg packets after each received
frame.
.TP
.B "\-s, \-\-subdirs, \-\-subdirectories"
Create SSRC/year/month/day subdirectories. Superseded by --jt and pipe modes.
.TP
.B "\-p, \-\-prefix\-source"
Prefix recording names with the sender socket identity.
.TP
.B "\-j, \-\-jt"
Use K1JT-style filenames; also enable boundary padding and clock-reset
handling.
.TP
.B "\-L seconds, \-\-length seconds, \-\-lengthlimit seconds, \-\-limit seconds"
Set file segment duration. Zero, the default, disables length-based
segmentation. --pad controls alignment and leading silence.
.TP
.B "\-x seconds, \-\-max_length seconds"
Currently an alias for --lengthlimit. It does not provide a separate one-shot
recording mode in this source revision.
.TP
.B "\-P, \-\-pad"
Align the first segment to a duration boundary and pad its beginning with
silence. Intended primarily for PCM.
.TP
.B "\-R, \-\-reset"
Enable clock-skew detection and reset handling.
.TP
.B "\-4, \-\-ft4"
Enable JT naming, padding, and reset handling with 7.5-second segments.
.TP
.B "\-8, \-\-ft8"
As above, with 15-second segments.
.TP
.B "\-w, \-\-wspr"
As above, with 120-second segments.
.TP
.B "\-m seconds, \-\-mintime seconds, \-\-minfiletime seconds"
Discard files whose qualifying recorded segments are too short. Default 0.2
seconds.
.TP
.B "\-t seconds, \-\-timeout seconds"
Close an idle session after this many seconds. Default 20; the argument is
parsed as an integer.
.TP
.B "\-l locale, \-\-locale locale"
Select locale; default LANG.
.TP
.B "\-v, \-\-verbose"
Increase diagnostics; may be repeated.
.TP
.B "\-V, \-\-version"
Print version and exit.
.SH COMMAND SUBSTITUTIONS
The --exec command supports $$ for a literal dollar sign, $c for channel
count, $d for frontend description, $f for encoding, $h/$k/$m for frequency in
Hz/kHz/MHz, $r for receiver sample rate in Hz, and $s for SSRC. Quote the
command with single quotes so the invoking shell does not expand these first.
.PP
The command is executed by a shell. Its input normally includes a WAVE header
for PCM, or an Ogg stream for Opus. Add --raw when a PCM consumer expects
samples without a header. --raw does not convert the sample encoding;
configure the radiod output to match the consumer.
.SH NOTES
Source identity and SSRC distinguish sessions. Recording starts only after
sufficient metadata is available. A sample-rate or format change can require a
new file/session. Pipe consumers must account for termination on idle or
session closure; --reacquire provides special continuity behavior only for
--stdout.
.SH EXAMPLES
Record into an existing directory, or save a selected raw float stream after
configuring its radiod encoding to f32le:
.PP
.nf
mkdir \-p recordings
pcmrecord \-d recordings hf\-pcm.local
pcmrecord \-\-stdout \-\-raw \-\-ssrc 10000000 hf\-pcm.local > samples.f32
pcmrecord \-\-exec 'decoder \-\-rate $r \-\-channels $c \-' hf\-pcm.local
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
radiod(8), monitor(1), wd-record(1), jt-decoded(1)
.PP
Project documentation: https://github.com/ka9q/ka9q-radio
